 Detail\n2004", "h": 0}, {"t": "i", "x": "https://chess-timeline.vercel.app/images/mixed/whiteread-chess.jpg", "c": "Rachel Whiteread Modern Chess Set — dollhouse furniture as pieces"}, {"t": "p", "x": "Rachel Whiteread\n2003", "h": 0}, {"t": "i", "x": "https://chess-timeline.vercel.app/images/mixed/hirst-chess2.jpg", "c": "Rachel Whiteread chess set detail — kitchen pieces"}, {"t": "p", "x": "Whiteread Detail\n2003", "h": 0}, {"t": "i", "x": "https://chess-timeline.vercel.app/images/mixed/hirst-chess3.jpg", "c": "Rachel Whiteread chess set — living room pieces"}, {"t": "p", "x": "Whiteread Pieces\n2003", "h": 0}, {"t": "i", "x": "https://chess-timeline.vercel.app/images/mixed/hirst-chess-correct.jpg", "c": "Damien Hirst Mental Escapology chess set — pharmacy cabinet"}, {"t": "p", "x": "Damien Hirst\n2003", "h": 0}, {"t": "i", "x": "https://chess-timeline.vercel.app/images/mixed/hirst-chess1.jpg", "c": "Damien Hirst Mental Escapology chess set detail"}, {"t": "p", "x": "Hirst Detail\n2003", "h": 0}, {"t": "i", "x": "https://chess-timeline.vercel.app/images/mixed/kruger-chess1.jpg", "c": "Barbara Kruger chess set"}, {"t": "p", "x": "Barbara Kruger\n2003", "h": 0}, {"t": "i", "x": "https://chess-timeline.vercel.app/images/mixed/kruger-chess2.jpg", "c": "Barbara Kruger chess set detail"}, {"t": "p", "x": "Kruger Detail\n2003", "h": 0}, {"t": "i", "x": "https://chess-timeline.vercel.app/images/mixed/emin-chess.jpg", "c": "Tracey Emin embroidered chess set"}, {"t": "p", "x": "Tracey Emin\n2003", "h": 0}], "text": "Ivory & Bone\nc. 700 AD – 1900\n\nAfrasiab Chessmen\nc. 700 AD\n\nCharlemagne Chessmen\nc. 1080–1120\n\nCharlemagne Knight\nc. 1080–1120\n\nCharlemagne Queen\nc. 1080–1120\n\nCharlemagne Pawn\nc. 1080–1120\n\nLewis Chessmen — Warder\nc. 1150–1200\n\nLewis Chessmen — Berserker\nc. 1150–1200\n\nLewis Chessmen — Group\nc. 1150–1200\n\nLewis Chessmen — Museum\nc. 1150–1200\n\nWalrus Ivory King\nMedieval\n\nWalrus Ivory Knight\nMedieval\n\nAfrasiab · Charlemagne · Lewis · Vizagapatam · Northern Upright\nThe oldest surviving chess pieces. From abstract 7th-century Samarkand to the expressive Lewis Chessmen of Norse origin, through ornate Indian export sets and Chinese puzzle balls. Ivory was the material of courts and conquerors.\n\nIvory Knight\nc. 1510\n\nVizagapatam Export Chess\nc. 1750–1850\n\nIndian Chessmen\nc. 1750–1850\n\nIndian Ivory Detail\nc. 1750–1850\n\nMet Chess Set\nc. 1800\n\nAger Chessmen\nc. 1050–1100\n\nChinese Puzzle Ball\n19th Century\n\nNorthern Upright\n19th Century\n\nNorthern Upright Slender\n19th Century\n\nPuzzle Ball — George II\nc. 1740s\n\nWood\n1616 – 2000\n\nSelenus Pattern\n1616\n\nSelenus Detail\n1616\n\nRégence Pattern\nc. 1715–1850\n\nBarleycorn Pattern\nc. 1800–1849\n\nStaunton Chess Set\n1849\n\nOriginal Jaques Staunton\n1849\n\nStaunton Opening\n1849\n\nMan Ray Chess Set\n1920–1926\n\nBauhaus Chess Set\n1924\n\nBauhaus — Walker Gallery\n1924\n\nHartwig Detail\n1924\n\nHartwig Pieces\n1924\n\nHartwig Complete\n1924\n\nMax Ernst\n1944\n\nSelenus · Staunton · Man Ray · Hartwig · Ernst · Calvert · St. George\nThe material of standardisation. From Selenus’s 1616 treatise through the Régence, St. George, and Barleycorn patterns to Nathaniel Cook’s 1849 Staunton, which became the universal standard. Then the modernists: Man Ray, Hartwig at the Bauhaus, Max Ernst.\n\nMax Ernst Pieces\n1944\n\nRégence Match\nc. 1715–1850\n\nJaques Staunton Original\n1849\n\nTournament Set\nWood\n\nMet Chess Board\nWood\n\nCalvert Chess\n19th Century\n\nCalvert Chessmen\n19th Century\n\nCalvert Board\n19th Century\n\nCalvert Pattern\n19th Century\n\nSt. George Chessmen\n19th Century\n\nSt. George Detail\n19th Century\n\nSt. George Men\n19th Century\n\nSt. George Style\n19th Century\n\nCeramic & Porcelain\n12th C – 1900s\n\nPersian Chess Set\nc. 12th Century\n\nPersian Rook\nc. 12th Century\n\nPersian Bishop\nc. 12th Century\n\nPersian King\nc. 12th Century\n\nPersian Knight\nc. 12th Century\n\nSasanian · Persian · Meissen · Kändler · Esser · Wedgwood\nFrom ancient Persian and Sasanian abstract forms, among the earliest known chess pieces, to Meissen under Kändler and Esser, and Wedgwood’s neoclassical jasperware. Clay was chess’s first medium; porcelain became its most precious.\n\nSasanian Agate\nAncient\n\nSasanian Ceramic\nAncient\n\nMeissen Seetiere\n1717–1930s\n\nMeissen Kändler\n18th Century\n\nWedgwood\nc. 1780s\n\nMetal\n1920 – present\n\nMan Ray — MoMA\n1920–1926\n\nAlexander Calder\nc. 1944\n\nBobby Fischer\n1972\n\nBobby Fischer\n1960\n\nFischer vs Spassky — G5\n1972\n\nFischer vs Spassky — G13\n1972\n\nFischer — Amsterdam\nMetal\n\nBobby Fischer\n1972\n\nGehry / Tiffany\n2012\n\nGehry / Tiffany Detail\n2012\n\nGehry / Tiffany Forms\n2012\n\nMan Ray · Calder · Fischer · Gehry · Hadid · Skyline Chess\nThe century of artists and architects. Man Ray’s geometric brass, Calder’s wire sculptures, Fischer’s legendary 1972 match against Spassky. Then Gehry for Tiffany, Zaha Hadid’s parametric towers, Skyline Chess’s city skylines cast in metal.\n\nGehry / Tiffany Pieces\n2012\n\nZaha Hadid\n2014\n\nZaha Hadid — Silver\n2014\n\nZaha Hadid — Red\n2014\n\nSkyline Chess — NYC\n2016–present\n\nSkyline Chess — Premium\n2016–present\n\nTournament Staunton\n1972\n\nCalder Detail\nc. 1944\n\nFischer–Spassky Set\n1972\n\nFischer–Spassky Detail\n1972\n\nFischer–Spassky Pieces\n1972\n\nCrystal & Glass\n1944 – 2014\n\nIsamu Noguchi\n1944\n\nNoguchi Chess Set\n1944\n\nNoguchi Table\n1944\n\nNoguchi Pieces\n1944\n\nNoguchi Set\n1944\n\nSwarovski Crystal\n1984–2007\n\nSwarovski Detail\n1984–2007\n\nMurano Glass\n1960s–present\n\nMurano Detail\n1960s–present\n\nNoguchi · Swarovski · Murano · Nendo / Baccarat\nTransparency as concept. Noguchi’s Lucite pieces for “The Imagery of Chess” exhibition, Swarovski’s precision-cut crystal, Murano’s hand-blown tradition, and Nendo’s radical reimagining of Baccarat’s Harcourt glass.\n\nMurano Pieces\n1960s–present\n\nNendo / Baccarat\n2014\n\nNendo / Baccarat Detail\n2014\n\nNendo / Baccarat Pieces\n2014\n\nNendo Sketch\n2014\n\nMixed & Conceptual\n1943 – present\n\nDalí — Fingers Chess\n1964–1968\n\nDalí — Board\n1964–1968\n\nDalí — Pieces\n1964–1968\n\nDalí — Detail\n1964–1968\n\nMarcel Duchamp\n1943\n\nDuchamp Playing\n1943\n\nDuchamp Pocket Chess\n1943\n\nYoko Ono — Play It By Trust\n1966\n\nYoko Ono — Detail\n1966\n\nLEGO Chess Set\n2012–present\n\nYoom Chess Set\nMixed & Conceptual\n\nYoom Chess\nMixed & Conceptual\n\nYoom Pieces\nMixed & Conceptual\n\nYoom Board\nMixed & Conceptual\n\nYoom Overview\nMixed & Conceptual\n\nYoom Close-up\nMixed & Conceptual\n\nPeter Ganine\nc. 1947\n\nMichael Graves\n2000\n\nGraves Detail\n2000\n\nGraves Pieces\n2000\n\nGraves Overview\n2000\n\nGraves Board\n2000\n\nDuchamp · Dalí · Ono · Hirst · Whiteread · Kruger · Emin · Kusama\nChess as art object. Duchamp quit art for chess. Dalí cast surreal fingers. Yoko Ono made both sides white. The RS&A “Art of Chess” commissions brought in Hirst, Whiteread, Kruger, and Emin. Plus Graves, Rashid, Hermès, Kusama, LEGO.\n\nGraves Complete\n2000\n\nHermès\n2014\n\nHermès Detail\n2014\n\nHermès Pieces\n2014\n\nKusama Pumpkin Chess\nMixed & Conceptual\n\nKusama Chess\nMixed & Conceptual\n\nKusama Detail\nMixed & Conceptual\n\nKusama Pieces\nMixed & Conceptual\n\nLEGO Detail\n2012–present\n\nLEGO Pieces\n2012–present\n\nLEGO Kingdoms\n2012–present\n\nLEGO Pirates\n2012–present\n\nKarim Rashid\n2004\n\nRashid Detail\n2004\n\nRachel Whiteread\n2003\n\nWhiteread Detail\n2003\n\nWhiteread Pieces\n2003\n\nDamien Hirst\n2003\n\nHirst Detail\n2003\n\nBarbara Kruger\n2003\n\nKruger Detail\n2003\n\nTracey Emin\n2003", "read_minutes": 4, "curated_at": "2026-09-20T11:12:33Z"}